Concise archival-safe sequence

1. Photograph the current position and mark the frame/package orientation. Work on a clean, dry, padded table with clean, dry hands; avoid cotton gloves, which can snag paper.
2. Lay the frame face-down. Remove the backing and hardware, then lift the mat/art package straight up—do not slide it. Keep the sheet supported by the mat or rigid backing.
3. Keep the watercolor face-up and avoid touching its image area. Do not pull on the sheet or peel a mount that is stuck to it.
4. If the corner pocket is intact and merely loose, lift only its outer flap and guide the sheet’s corner back into it using a clean strip of smooth, acid-free interleaving paper. Do not use a bone folder or tool against the watercolor.
5. If replacement is needed, use a new acid-free, lignin-free rag-paper corner or inert polyester corner. Attach it only to the rag mounting board/backing with wheat-starch paste or methylcellulose—never to the sheet. Apply any paste with the artwork removed, let it dry completely under light restraint, and check that it is fully dry before inserting the sheet.
6. Reassemble with a spacer so the glazing cannot touch the watercolor. Confirm that the backing does not compress the corners or bow the mat; tighten hardware evenly but gently.

Do not use pressure-sensitive tape, double-sided tape, glue sticks, rubber cement, or household adhesive. Do not introduce moisture near the mounted sheet.

Stop handling it at home if:

- the mount is bonded, brittle, stained, or leaves adhesive residue;
- the sheet resists movement, begins to curl, cockle, tear, or buckle;
- pigment transfers, flakes, becomes shiny, or appears friable;
- there is foxing, tide staining, mold, insect damage, or active dampness;
- the sheet is stuck to the glazing or the glazing has touched the paint;
- correcting the alignment would require flattening, pulling, peeling, or applying moisture.

In those cases, close the package without forcing it and have a paper conservator handle the removal or remounting.
